Install it as a service, and stop putting a window in your face

This is meant to give local projects frontier inference, which means it has
to be installable and quiet. Two changes.

Code and state are now different directories. ROOT is where the code lives;
HOME is where config, the Chrome profiles holding your logins, downloads,
the ledger and exports live. They were the same, which is fine for a
checkout and wrong the moment `npm i -g` puts the code under npm's tree -
your logins would be buried there and lost on the next reinstall. A
checkout with config.json next to the code keeps behaving exactly as
before; anything else uses %LOCALAPPDATA%\uibridge. config.json is no
longer shipped inside the package for the same reason (it would make the
install directory look like a home); config.example.json ships instead.
`uibridge paths` prints all of it, and `uibridge autostart` installs a
logon task so any program on the machine can just call the API.

Window mode turned out to be two questions, not one. "Headless" and "no
window in my face" are different, and the difference is measured: with
--headless=new, chatgpt.com returns an ANONYMOUS session even though the
profile's __Secure-next-auth.session-token.0/.1 are present and sent - so
uibridge refuses to send, correctly, and headless is simply not available
there. Gemini works headless fine. So there are three modes: true,
'offscreen' (a real browser parked off the visible desktop - nothing
appears, and the site sees what a person's browser looks like), and false.
Each provider's default is the one that was verified live: gemini true,
chatgpt offscreen. Login always opens a real visible window, because a
human types there.

Also learned while measuring: a headless and a headed Chrome on the SAME
profile directory yield a browser with the cookies present and no session.
That, not headless, was the first signed_out I saw.

